1. Overview
Our application uses Access Manager for Web (AMFW) for authentication.
AMFW enforces strict security checks and prevents proxy-based recording of login flows. As a result, JMeter cannot directly record or replay the login sequence.
This SOP outlines two options:
2. Before You Begin — Confirm Whether SSO Can Be Disabled
🔹 Option A — Preferred (If Allowed): Request SSO Bypass for Testing
If possible, coordinate with the application team / DevOps team to:
Temporarily disable AMFW authentication for the test environment,
OR provide a direct non-SSO login endpoint,
OR enable a test-only authentication bypass,
OR allow the use of static session tokens for test users.
This is the cleanest approach because:
✔ The login flow becomes recordable
✔ Tokens do not need manual refresh
✔ Authentication can be load-tested independently
✔ Simplifies scripting and correlation significantly
If the development team can implement an SSO bypass, use that method instead of this document.
3. If SSO Cannot Be Disabled — Use This Procedure
If the AMFW login cannot be bypassed (most common scenario), follow the steps below.
This approach avoids interacting with AMFW entirely by using manual cookie extraction and post-login-only recording.
4. Step-by-Step Process
STEP 1 — Manually Log In (JMeter Proxy OFF)
Ensure the JMeter Recorder (proxy) is not running.
Open your browser normally.
Navigate to the application.
Authenticate using Access Manager for Web.
Stop when you reach the main authenticated landing page.
Your browser now holds valid AMFW session cookies.
STEP 2 — Capture the Access Manager Session Cookies
Press F12 to open DevTools
Go to Application → Cookies (Chrome) or Storage → Cookies (Firefox)
Select the authenticated domain
Identify cookies created after login, such as:
iPlanetDirectoryPro
AMSESSIONID
AMAuthCookie
JSESSIONID (app-side session)
Any custom session tokens specific to our app
If unsure, copy all cookies for the domain.
STEP 3 — Insert Cookies into JMeter
Thread Group → Add → Config Element → HTTP Cookie Manager
Add each cookie with:
Name
Value
Domain (e.g., .fematdl.net)
Path (/ for most cookies)
Secure flag — must match the browser
Set:
❗ Disable
Clear cookies each iteration
(Otherwise, JMeter deletes the authenticated session)
Example:
Name	Value	Domain	Path
iPlanetDirectoryPro	ABC123	.fematdl.net	/
JSESSIONID	XYZ987	app.fematdl.net	/
STEP 4 — Record or Create Only Post-Login Requests
Do not attempt to record:
Access Manager login pages
AMFW redirects
Authentication handlers
SAML-like token exchanges
These will fail and are not required for our tests.
Record or build only post-login functionality, such as:
Dashboard
Navigation
Form submissions
API/Servlet interactions
Search and retrieve operations
These can be captured by:
✔ JMeter Recorder (after login only)
✔ Manual HTTP Request creation
5. Session Expiration
AMFW sessions have timeouts—idle or absolute.
When expired:
JMeter will redirect to login
Cookies must be refreshed
Repeat Steps 1–3
Plan accordingly for long-running or high-user-count tests.
6. Best Practices for Our AMFW Authentication Flow
✔ Extract cookies immediately after login
✔ Copy Secure/Domain/Path flags exactly
✔ Always test with “Preserve Log” enabled in DevTools
✔ Avoid recording or simulating AMFW login flows
✔ Use correlation for dynamic POST parameters (e.g., sessionId)
✔ Maintain a pool of test users if possible
7. Summary
If the development team can disable or bypass Access Manager authentication:
➡️ Use the bypass—it is the preferred and simplest method.
If AMFW cannot be bypassed:
➡️ Follow this SOP:
Manual login
Extract cookies
Insert cookies into JMeter
Record only post-login requests
This approach is stable, secure, and aligned with how our authentication mechanism works.
